WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.540
Hello, my name
is Colin Rundel.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:08.650
And today, I'm going to
tell you a little bit

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:10.025
about a package
I've been working

00:00:10.025 --> 00:00:12.100
on recently called parsermd.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.250
This package implements a parser
for R Markdown documents using

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:17.800
the boost spirit
library and C++,

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.740
and exposes a series of
functions and data structures

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:24.340
that allow for the manipulation
and evaluation of R Markdown

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:26.320
documents from within R.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.400
So to give you a
little bit of context,

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.760
I teach large statistical
computing courses.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.650
And I deliver
assignments to students

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.470
in the form of R
Markdown documents.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:37.880
These R Markdown documents
have very typical structures,

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:41.170
so they have things like YAML at
the beginning, section headings

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:44.170
to keep bits and pieces
organized, instructions

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:47.650
in the form of Markdown
text, and then various code

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:49.793
chunks that include
either example code

00:00:49.793 --> 00:00:51.460
or areas where we
want students to enter

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:53.290
their own code to
implement a solution

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:54.730
or something like that.

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:56.470
And this works
really, really well

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.120
except one of the
difficulties we have

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.760
is that the documents
contain lots of boilerplate

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.973
in the form of these
instructions or demo code

00:01:02.973 --> 00:01:05.140
that we don't actually care
about when it comes time

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:05.640
to mark it.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.630
And actually, it
distracts from the content

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.310
that we are really
concerned with.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:10.960
And so part of the
motivating reason

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.120
for developing the
parsermd package

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.617
was a desire to be able to
take these kind of documents

00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:17.200
once the students
have turned them in,

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:19.240
and then strip out all
of that boilerplate

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:22.028
to get down to just the stuff
that we actually care about.

00:01:22.028 --> 00:01:24.070
And so today, I'll show
you some examples of what

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:26.300
that looks like in practice.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:30.310
So at its core is this
parse_rmd function,

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:32.270
which reads an R
Markdown document.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:35.470
And then turns it into a
simple abstract syntax tree.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:36.980
And so for our
purposes right now,

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:38.980
we don't really need to
understand what that is.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:42.160
But the key thing to see is all
of those elements we just saw

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.360
in that homework
01.rmd are here.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:46.850
So we see that there's
the YAML is represented.

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:48.670
We have the various
section headings.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:51.640
We have the markdown
text and the code chunks.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:53.557
And this is presented
in a hierarchical way,

00:01:53.557 --> 00:01:54.640
but it's really just flat.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:56.080
We're just inferring
the hierarchy

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.300
based on the various
heading levels

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:01.300
that we see within the document.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.410
If this hierarchical view
doesn't work for you,

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:07.420
we can also turn this into
a tidy rectangle of data

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:08.930
using the as_tibble function.

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:11.170
And so what we now have
is the same representation

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:13.985
of that data where the AST has
been wrapped up into a tibble,

00:02:13.985 --> 00:02:15.610
and we can manipulate
things by looking

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.443
at the various other
columns that are there.

00:02:17.443 --> 00:02:20.180
But that AST we were just
seeing is still here.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:23.320
It's now just in its flat form
represented in that ast column

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:25.430
on the right hand side.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:27.432
So AST, what is that?

00:02:27.432 --> 00:02:28.390
How do I work with that?

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:29.080
What do I care about it?

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:30.955
It's a custom data
structure, but it's really

00:02:30.955 --> 00:02:33.010
just a list of lists.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.427
And it's all managed by
S3, and it's not something

00:02:35.427 --> 00:02:37.510
that you actually need to
worry about in practice.

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:40.400
Because the package implements
a bunch of helper functions.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.220
So if we want a subset
for various elements

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:45.490
on the example I have,
I care about the exercises

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:46.307
and the solutions.

00:02:46.307 --> 00:02:47.890
So here, I can use
the subset function

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:49.870
to pull out anything
that matches

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:52.580
exercises with a wildcard
and then solution.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:54.520
So this gets me all
of the nodes that

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:56.952
belong to an exercise
one, two, or three,

00:02:56.952 --> 00:02:58.660
and then the various
answers the students

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:00.940
may have put into
those particular bits

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:01.970
of the document.

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:03.820
I can pull those out.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:05.320
Once I've done that,
I can then also

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:07.960
manipulate the document further,
so a common issue students

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:10.237
have as they may write
code that has an error.

00:03:10.237 --> 00:03:12.070
And if we were to try
to knit that document,

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:14.463
we would not be able to,
because of the error.

00:03:14.463 --> 00:03:16.630
So what we can do is we can
then manipulate the code

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:17.930
chunks to add new options.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.470
So this is going to go through,
and set error equal to true

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:21.730
for all of the code chunks.

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:22.900
So that when we
knit the document,

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:24.340
it'll keep knitting
even if there

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:27.525
is an error at any
point in the document.

00:03:27.525 --> 00:03:28.900
Once we've done
that, we can then

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:31.330
say turn the document
back in text,

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:33.500
or we can even just
render it directly.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:36.110
So in this case, I can turn
it back into an HTML document

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:36.958
and examine it.

00:03:36.958 --> 00:03:38.500
And so again, this
makes it very easy

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:41.458
to then take the student's code,
pull out the pieces I want,

00:03:41.458 --> 00:03:43.000
and then render just
the solution, so

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.400
that I can then mark it much
more easily without all

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:48.310
of that other sort
of cruft around.

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:49.600
This works really well.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.010
In practice, it's a
little bit more difficult,

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:53.110
because we depend on the
structure of the document

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:56.000
matching exactly what we expect
when we're extracting things.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:57.160
And so one of the
things that I've also

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:58.535
done in practice
with this that's

00:03:58.535 --> 00:04:00.580
been able to be built
on top of these tools

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:02.743
is the idea of a template,
an automatic feedback

00:04:02.743 --> 00:04:03.410
to the students.

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:06.250
So an RMD template is just
a simple representation

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:08.590
of the nodes that
we expect to find

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:11.530
and the various sort of
labels and location references

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:13.780
that we care about
indicate where they are.

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:15.490
And it's just a
simple tibble that we

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:17.620
can generate using the
rmd_template function

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:19.779
once we subsetting for
the elements we want.

00:04:19.779 --> 00:04:21.250
And once we have
that, we can then

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:23.770
feed it into another helper
function called rmd_check_template,

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:26.410
which we can then use
that to compare

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:27.610
against the student's work.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:30.610
So here, I'm comparing
against hw01-student,

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:32.440
and we get a user-friendly
error message

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.040
out telling you
exactly what's missing

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:35.790
and what's problematic
about the document.

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:37.840
So in this case, the
student is missing something

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:39.590
in exercise one
and exercise two,

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:41.590
and they're told explicitly
what that is, either

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:44.050
a code chunk or markdown text
or what it's particularly

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:45.725
looking for in that context.

00:04:45.725 --> 00:04:47.350
And so this is
something we're actually

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:50.000
able to deploy to students this
semester via GitHub Actions.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:52.000
And if it's something
that you're interested in,

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:54.190
all of the example code
including the GitHub Action

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:55.990
workflows are
included in the repo

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:58.200
that I've linked at
the bottom there.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:59.640
Thank you very
much for watching.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.098
Additional details
about the package

00:05:01.098 --> 00:05:02.730
are available on
GitHub, and please

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:05.760
let us know if you have any
feedback, if the package breaks

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:07.380
or you have an
interesting use case.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.930
Thanks!

